<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/012X">Remark <span data-tag="012X">3.1.8.9</span>.</a> Corollary <a data-tag="012V" href="/tag/012V">3.1.8.7</a> and Variant <a data-tag="012W" href="/tag/012W">3.1.8.8</a> can be stated more informally as follows: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
The homotopy category $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{Kan}}$ can be obtained from the category $\operatorname{Kan}$ of Kan complexes by formally adjoining inverses to all homotopy equivalences. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The homotopy category $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{Kan}}$ can be obtained from the category $\operatorname{Set_{\Delta }}$ of simplicial sets by formally adjoining inverses to all weak homotopy equivalences. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
<p> Either of these assertions characterizes the homotopy category $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{Kan}}$ up to equivalence (in fact, Corollary <a data-tag="012V" href="/tag/012V">3.1.8.7</a> even characterizes $\operatorname {h}\! \mathit{\operatorname{Kan}}$ up to <em>isomorphism</em>). </p>
</article>
